Part I — Theory

1) Value Types vs Reference Types (storage)
- Value types (structs, primitives like int, bool, char) store the actual value. They are typically allocated on the stack or inlined inside objects/arrays. Copying a value type copies its data; each copy is independent.
- Reference types (classes, arrays, delegates) store a reference to an object on the heap. Copying a reference copies the reference (pointer); multiple references can point to the same heap object. The heap object lifetime is managed by the garbage collector.

2) Init-only properties (C# 9/10) vs normal set
- init-only properties allow assignment only during object initialization (in the constructor or object initializer). Once initialization completes, the properties become effectively immutable.
- Normal set accessors allow mutation at any time.
- Use-case: create immutable DTOs or configuration objects that can be initialized via object initializers but should not be modified later.

3) virtual vs override (polymorphism)
- virtual (base class): marks a method as overridable and provides a default implementation.
- override (derived class): replaces the base virtual implementation. At runtime, calls are dispatched to the most-derived override (dynamic dispatch), enabling polymorphic behavior.

4) Why static members are not accessed through an instance
- static members belong to the type itself, not to any particular instance. They represent shared, type-level state or behavior. Allowing instance access would be misleading: there is no per-instance copy. Language design requires accessing static members through the type to make intent clear.
